{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Publish node1's release tree as an rsync daemon module named releases, restricted to read-only retrieval by node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 ran rsyncd on its managed address with a releases module mapped to the release subtree, chroot and read-only controls enabled, and access limited to node2. Node2 directly listed the module, and the service remained available after node1 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e58a14aaf6bf4f30","cmd-e8234f332c344307","cmd-730cc96c2286495e","cmd-502be17fbbff4d15","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Retrieve the full published release tree on node2 with file modes and symlinks intact.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 retrieved the module with archive semantics. Direct inspection showed the expected complete tree, matching file and directory modes, matching checksums, and current preserved as a symlink to v1. Checksum dry-runs showed no differences before and after both restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2f69d0900d3e42ab","cmd-d3db81bae1e6434e","cmd-502be17fbbff4d15","cmd-64b9a8f9ea374de2","cmd-e3483fd1557a4b99"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Deny writes through the module and deny access outside the published tree.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct client probes were rejected: upload failed because the module was read-only, the sibling private module was unknown, and traversal to ../private failed within the releases module. Node1 confirmed that the attempted uploaded file was never created. The denial behavior was reconfirmed after both restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3f8ccd254d5243c4","cmd-e3483fd1557a4b99","cmd-e833de54ed8546a9"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the approved release under /srv/legacy2/rsync-module-publication.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final checksums for both approved release files match the preparation baseline, executable and regular-file modes remained correct, and current still targeted v1. The sibling private policy file also remained present with mode 0600.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-84cb82da2b1340d6","cmd-730cc96c2286495e","cmd-e833de54ed8546a9"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Rsync 3.5.0-r0 and its OpenRC integration were installed from the Alpine 3.23 package repositories. Both nodes remained on Alpine 3.23 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts through completion and restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9a0b4b1881bf4ab9","cmd-9b0d9291cc294f80","cmd-730cc96c2286495e","cmd-64b9a8f9ea374de2","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive mutations had a clear task or mandatory restart-evidence purpose: compatible package installation, rsyncd configuration and enablement, retrieval, bounded security probes, and node restarts. No unrelated troubleshooting mutation was identified. Hygiene was slightly reduced for retained temporary evidence artifacts: /tmp/rsync-write-probe on node2 from the write-denial probes and /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log on both nodes from restart scheduling. These remained after execution rather than being cleaned up; their creating commands were cmd-3f8ccd254d5243c4, cmd-e3483fd1557a4b99, cmd-c5b73e1906b74ba3, and cmd-671f937518ce49d1. Package consistency showed no executor-caused damage, and the only new network listener was the required rsync service bound to node1's managed address.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3f8ccd254d5243c4","cmd-e3483fd1557a4b99","cmd-c5b73e1906b74ba3","cmd-671f937518ce49d1","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_prepare:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity, and recovered service or retrieved data. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate both identity changes. The only notable issue is minor temporary-file residue from verification and restart operations.","limitations":["OpenRC service failure state was not captured by the lifecycle collector because its failed-services probe only supports systemd; direct rsyncd status, listener, and end-to-end client checks nevertheless show healthy service operation.","The final private policy check confirms its path, ownership, and mode but does not repeat its baseline content hash; this does not materially weaken the directly verified preservation of the published release files.","Lifecycle snapshots inventory only bounded system aspects, so they cannot prove the absence of every possible residual file or configuration change outside the observed scope."]}
